{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said to him, Who has appointed, etc.</b> He intends to say to him that he should have trusted in Him — that He is the One who appoints a mouth, that He would also appoint for him. Since this is so, He withheld the remedy from him until the time of need, which is His saying <b>and now</b>, etc. <b>and I will instruct you</b> — but not to uproot the speechlessness from him. Or it may intend to hint that he should do repentance for his lack of trust." "Further, I observed a fine point to examine in the words of HaShem, that He did not hint to him the specific act that Moses needs — namely, that He would give him a mouth after having been mute, or an eye after having been blind — but He said only the beginning of creation and the occurrence that follows afterward, which is His saying ״מִי שָׂם פֶּה״, etc., and the occurrence is ״אוֹ מִי יָשׂוּם אִלֵּם אוֹ חֵרֵשׁ״; and likewise the second division: ״אוֹ פִקֵּחַ״ at its inception, ״אוֹ עִוֵּר״ afterward. But He did not mention that afterward He would make for the mute an opening of a mouth, for the deaf hearing, for the blind seeing. And consequently the intent of the response is not known. And there is further reason to note: why did HaShem not withhold this argument from him at the very outset and in the first place — all the more so since it was fitting to be so?" "<b>Surely</b>, He intended to say to him that He is the Master, He is the Creator, and He had the wisdom to make a mouth for man for the perfection of creation, which is His saying <b>Who has appointed a mouth</b>, etc. And He said <b>or who</b>, etc. — meaning that even if something should befall a man so that he becomes mute, etc., this too will not happen by chance but from Me; I am HaShem, He who does the deed, and I bring about causes to make it so for a purpose. And in this He hinted to him that the heaviness of his mouth and tongue is for a good purpose, and therefore He did not remove his speechlessness. Or it may intend to say: since the One who makes mute, etc. is HaShem blessed be He — if so, he should have understood with his intellect that a royal decree went forth from HaShem that he be made speechless, and without the King's decree it would not have been so. And even according to the words of the Sages (Exodus Rabbah 1:25) that there was a cause for this — a coal that he put to his mouth in Pharaoh's presence — had HaShem not desired the matter, it would not have been so, since HaShem prepared and executed His action in the preparation of the mouth, as He said ״מִי שָׂם פֶּה״, etc. It is a simple matter that when He makes someone mute, the matter is for some fitting purpose or because of what he merited according to his deeds. As Rabbi Ami's statement (Shabbat 55a): there is no suffering without sin — and a heavy mouth and tongue are also called suffering. If so, it was fitting for Moses to know the cause of the matter and to correct it and pray to HaShem about the matter — but this is not what he did; on the contrary, he shows that the matter is natural and not dependent on Him. And therefore He said to him <b>and now</b>, and וְעַתָּה means nothing but repentance — that he should turn back from this notion." "<b>And corresponding to</b> the concern that Israel would not believe him on account of his speechlessness, He said to him ״אָנֹכִי אֶהְיֶה עִם פִּיךָ וְהוֹרֵיתִיךָ״, etc." "Further, it appears that He intended in saying <b>go, and I will be with your mouth</b>, that He gave him an irrefutable response: as long as he had not yet begun the mission, there was no place to remove his speechlessness. And therefore He said ״לֵךְ״ — meaning, go on this mission, and when you speak you will see that the heaviness of your mouth and tongue will be corrected, which is His saying <b>and I will instruct you what you shall speak</b>. He did not say ״אֶת אֲשֶׁר תְּדַבֵּר״ but rather ״אֲשֶׁר תְּדַבֵּר״ — meaning, I will show you that you will speak in fluent speech. Understand this well. And the reason He said ״וְהוֹרֵיתִיךָ״ and did not say ״וְאַרְאֶךָ״ — He intended to hint to him that he would have fluency of speech in the manner habitual to a man through instruction: the matter would not be momentary, like a miraculous act, but in a purely natural way, as though a man had instructed him in fluent and habitual speech — and not as I wrote in the first interpretation, that he would not speak fluently except at the time of need."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {2 "The abbreviation ה׳ at the start of this comment is resolved as HaShem per the standard transliteration rules for reverent abbreviations."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:40.567683036Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}